course: dictionary
lesson: 9067
title: Slimy and what it tells us
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Slimy means covered with slime: thick, wet, slippery stuff, like the slime on a snail or a slug. «A banana skin is slippery because its squashed inside makes a slimy layer between your shoe and the floor.» The clue slippery tells you that slimy can mean slippery and wet.
explain: Slime often helps living things. «A clownfish is safe because a layer of slimy mucus covers its skin.» The slime keeps the fish's skin wet and protects it. Slugs, snails and frogs also feel slimy, and their slime helps them move.
explain: Slimy can also describe a person. When someone is too friendly in a false way to get what they want, we call them slimy. This use is informal, so you will hear it in talk and stories more than in science books. Read the words around it to tell which meaning fits.
fact: Slimy means covered with slime, a thick, wet, slippery stuff.
fact: «A snake's skin is dry and smooth, not slimy.» So snakes are not slimy, even though their skin looks shiny.
fact: Slime on a snail or a slug helps it move and keeps its skin wet.
fact: Slimy can also mean too friendly in a false way to get what you want. This meaning is informal.
fact: Phrases you may meet are slimy slug and slimy rocks.
exercise: «A snake's skin is dry and smooth, not slimy.» What does slimy mean? | Hard and bumpy like a shell | Hot and dusty | *Covered with thick, wet slime | Dry and smooth
exercise: «A banana skin is slippery because its squashed inside makes a slimy layer between your shoe and the floor.» Why is a banana skin slippery? | *Because of a slimy layer between your shoe and the floor | Because it is hard and dry | Because it is hot from the sun
exercise: «A clownfish is safe because a layer of slimy mucus covers its skin.» Copy the word that tells you the mucus is wet and slippery. | safe | *slimy | covers | layer
test: «The shop assistant was slimy, all smiles and compliments, just to get our money.» What does slimy mean here? | Cold and clean | Covered with wet slime | Very tired and sleepy | *Too friendly in a false way, to get what they want
test: Which of these is a living thing with slimy skin? | A snake | A dry, smooth stone | A cactus | *A slug
exam: If the words around slimy do not tell you what it means, what should you do? | Ask the nearest pond | Guess and move on without checking | Ignore the word and never read that sentence again | *Check the word in a dictionary
